# tools/cr/crypto_elligator_key_pair_witness.rish -- the hidden keypair (crypto_elligator_key_pair) authored in pure Rye: Season G's composition
# that closes the Elligator key-hiding arc end to end. crypto/elligator.rye maps a curve point to a uniformly random representative and back, and
# crypto/x25519_dirty.rye produces a public key ranging over the whole curve so its representative is uniform; this rung marries the two behind
# one door -- from a 32-byte seed, a fresh X25519 secret key and the random-looking representative of its dirty public key, the key Comlink's
# obfuscated first packet carries (Season G - Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G names reimplementing Monocypher
# primitives in Rye with byte-for-byte parity as the crux; the x25519_dirty rung named this composition as its next crux).
# Kin: crypto/elligator.rye (the map/rev this drives) - crypto/x25519_dirty.rye (the dirty public key it hides) - crypto/chacha20.rye (the
# keystream that turns one seed into each candidate secret) - crypto/fe25519.rye (the base field all three stand on). Monocypher
# (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line -- our own composition, studied clean-room from Monocypher's public
# crypto_elligator_key_pair API (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/elligator_key_pair.rye authors key_pair(seed) as the loop Monocypher's crypto_elligator_key_pair runs, step for step: seed the working
# buffer's high half, ChaCha-stream the whole buffer keyed by that half (counter 0, zero nonce -- where the IETF block coincides with Monocypher's
# DJB block), take the dirty X25519 public key of the low half, and try to hide it with Elligator rev under the high half's first byte as tweak; a
# representable key ends the bounded loop, an unrepresentable one leaves its fresh ChaCha bytes to seed the next try. It authors no new
# arithmetic -- a composition over three GREEN rungs. The selftest proves the composition is CLOSED (map(hidden) = dirty_small(secret) across
# twenty-four seeds -- the representative truly hides the key), deterministic in its seed, and non-trivial. The byte-for-byte external anchor lives
# in tools/cr/crypto_monocypher_elligator_key_pair_parity_witness.rish, which builds the ACTUAL vendored Monocypher and proves our secret_key and
# hidden equal its crypto_elligator_key_pair over sixteen seeds. Purely local -- no key, no network, no funds, no device; the retry loop is bounded
# and constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_elligator_key_pair_witness.rish

say "crypto-elligator-key-pair: Language EN — the hidden keypair authored in pure Rye, the composition that closes the Elligator key-hiding arc end to end. From a 32-byte seed it derives a fresh X25519 secret key, takes its dirty public key (ranging over the whole curve), and hides it as a uniformly random 32-byte representative a network observer cannot tell from noise — the key Comlink's obfuscated first packet carries."
say "crypto-elligator-key-pair: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively as one bounded loop reproducing Monocypher's own: seed the working buffer's high half, ChaCha-stream it into a candidate key, take that key's dirty public key, and try to hide it; a representable key ends the loop, an unrepresentable one feeds the next try. Authored over three GREEN rungs — elligator, x25519_dirty, chacha20 — with no new arithmetic, so the same seed always yields the same keypair anyone can replay against the vendored library."
say "crypto-elligator-key-pair: Lens TAME — the composition calls no std.crypto; it bounds the retry loop at a named maximum and fails with a named error rather than spinning. The selftest proves the composition is closed (map(hidden) = dirty_small(secret)), deterministic, and non-trivial; the byte-for-byte Monocypher-source parity in tools/cr/crypto_monocypher_elligator_key_pair_parity_witness.rish is the external anchor, since Zig ships no Elligator keypair. Monocypher is the parity target, linked never copied. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/elligator_key_pair"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/elligator_key_pair.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-elligator-key-pair: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/elligator_key_pair.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-elligator-key-pair: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-elligator-key-pair: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-elligator-key-pair' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-elligator-key-pair: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-elligator-key-pair line"

say "GREEN crypto-elligator-key-pair: crypto/elligator_key_pair.rye builds and its selftest proves the key-hiding composition closed (map(hidden) = dirty_small(secret)), deterministic, and non-trivial; the byte-for-byte Monocypher-source parity stands in tools/cr/crypto_monocypher_elligator_key_pair_parity_witness.rish. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device; constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon."
